  <h2>Make replenishment part of the display plan</h2>
  <p>A display can look attractive on launch day and become confusing after a few sales. Plan how staff will replace products, recognise empty spaces and refresh the assortment. A simple plan shared with the store team helps prevent a product in the stockroom from staying absent from the shelf. Price and product information should remain visible throughout these changes.</p>
  <p>Match the selection to the space actually available. Adding a licence does not mean adding every reference. A compact, clearly presented and monitored range may be easier to manage than a scattered assortment. Our <a href="{{ path('app_article_wholesaler.en') }}">guide to choosing a manga drinks wholesaler</a> covers packaging, delivery and availability questions to ask before buying.</p>
  <h2>Measure tests with the right indicators</h2>
  <p>Compare periods of equal length and note unusual events such as promotions, holidays, late deliveries or stockouts. Track units sold and available stock, followed by revenue and margin where your data allows it. Increased revenue alone is not proof of profitability. Purchasing costs, discounts, transport and potential losses also need to be considered.</p>
  <p>Start with one observable change, such as grouping a family or improving signage. Collect staff feedback too: recurring questions, misunderstood products and requests for characters you do not carry. These observations create a useful basis for your next conversation with a <a href="{{ path('app_wholesale.en') }}">professional drinks and snacks wholesaler</a>, without assuming identical growth in every store.</p>
